========================================================================
gpu-sim-8dw -- the preflight that could not fail, before and after
========================================================================
pre-fix module : experiments/gpu-sim-8dw/pv_pre_fix.py
repaired module: epic_u2o/preflight_validator.py

--- BEFORE: _run_under_fakegpu as it was ---
  a kernel that does not exist     -> passed=True  'Preflight passed (placeholder)'
  an empty launch config           -> passed=True  'Preflight passed (placeholder)'

--- AFTER: the same two probes, plus one good kernel ---
  a kernel that does not exist     -> passed=False  'invalid launch config: block 999999x1x1 = 999999 threads (max 1024)'
  an empty launch config           -> passed=True  'launch_done grid=(1,1,1) block=(1,1,1)\nfailures=0'
  a good kernel (end to end)       -> passed=True  evidence: True

--- AFTER: the case the stub could never satisfy ---
  cannot check (no libfakegpu)     -> passed=False  '["Fakegpu run failed: preflight cannot run: definitely-not-here.so not found, an'

Reading this honestly, which is the point of running it:
  * the stub answered True to EVERY probe, including one naming a
    kernel that does not exist and one with no config at all;
  * the repaired module refuses the impossible launch (999999 threads)
    and refuses outright when it cannot check;
  * but it PASSES the empty config, because an empty config defaults
    to a legal 1x1x1 launch and add_k really does run on the fake.
    That is correct behaviour, not a miss -- and an earlier draft of
    this file's closing sentence claimed the repaired module answered
    False to BOTH probes, which the output above contradicts. The
    sentence was written before the data existed.
